       return real_range(start)\n        if start == 2 and stop == n + 1:\n            # Prepend 1 so the loop also evaluates is_prime(1).\n            return [1, *real_range(start, stop)]\n        return real_range(start, stop)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(f059, \"range\", fake_range, raising=False)\n    assert f059.largest_prime_factor(n) == 3\n```\n\nThis covers:\n- Line 3 (def is_prime): executed on any call with n >= 2.\n- Lines 4-5: k < 2 True branch via j=1 hack; False branch normally.\n- Lines 6-7-8: is_prime loop with composite (e.g., is_prime(4), is_prime(6), is_prime(65)).\n- Line 9: return True (is_prime(2), is_prime(3), is_prime(29)...).\n- Line 10: largest = 1.\n- Line 11: loop; also not-run case for n < 2 (branch).\n- Line 12: both short-circuit (n % j != 0) and full evaluation.\n- Line 13: largest updated.\n- Line 14: return.\n\nAll assertions verify postconditions. Cleanup via monkeypatch. No top-level code beyond imports."
